summary: |
  TEST-M1-STORY-095 (forgot password via email/SMS, set new password) is **already implemented
  and tested** on this branch: `src/auth/store.js`, `src/auth/routes.js`,
  `src/auth/notifyClient.js`, `public/forgot-password.html` + `public/js/forgot-password.js`,
  `public/sign-in.html` + `public/js/sign-in.js`, `public/css/auth.css`, wired into
  `src/server.js` at `/auth`. Every one of AC1-AC10 has an existing passing test
  (`test/auth.test.js`, `test/auth-store.test.js`, `test/forgot-password.test.js`,
  `test/sign-in.test.js`) and the screens/flow match the approved prototype at
  `.arc/designs/TEST-M1-STORY-095-design.html` (request -> generic confirm -> OTP-or-link ->
  set-new-password -> success -> sign-in banner). While verifying that implementation against
  the design and the AC text, one real functional defect was found: the "one-time code" sent for
  the SMS/phone channel is not actually a short code at all, so the SMS path as built can never
  work end-to-end outside of tests that bypass the UI. This plan's only new work is a test-first
  fix for that defect; everything else is verification that the existing tests/code still satisfy
  each AC, not new build-out.
scope:
  - description: |
      Fix the SMS/phone "one-time code" so it is actually a short, user-typeable code, not a
      full UUID token.

      Today `requestPasswordReset` in `src/auth/store.js` always does
      `const credential = crypto.randomUUID();` regardless of channel, then for the phone branch
      calls `notifyClient.sendResetSms({ to: contact, credential, ttlMinutes })` — i.e. the
      "SMS code" the user is told to enter is a 36-character UUID like
      `3fa85f64-5717-4562-b3fc-2c963f66afa6`. But the approved design's OTP screen
      (`.arc/designs/TEST-M1-STORY-095-design.html`, screen `data-name="Enter reset code"`,
      element `#otp-input`) and the already-built `public/forgot-password.html` /
      `public/js/forgot-password.js` OTP screen (`data-name="otp"`, `#otp-input`) both
      hard-restrict entry to 6 digits:
      `<input ... id="otp-input" type="text" inputmode="numeric" maxlength="6" .../>` plus
      `otpInput.addEventListener('input', () => { otpInput.value = otpInput.value.replace(/\D/g, '').slice(0, 6); })`.
      A 6-digit numeric string typed into that field can never equal the stored UUID credential,
      so `POST /auth/reset/verify` / `/auth/reset/confirm` will always report
      `invalid_or_expired` for a real phone-channel reset. This currently goes unnoticed because
      the backend tests for the phone channel in `test/auth.test.js` only assert that
      `notifyClient.sendResetSms` was called (AC2) — none of them simulate the user actually
      typing the delivered code back in — and the frontend tests in `test/forgot-password.test.js`
      use a `fakeApi` that never touches the real credential format.

      Fix: generate a channel-appropriate credential in `requestPasswordReset(contact)` —
      keep `crypto.randomUUID()` for the `email` channel (used as an opaque link token), and for
      the `phone` channel generate a 6-digit numeric string with
      `String(crypto.randomInt(0, 1_000_000)).padStart(6, '0')`. Both are stored under the same
      `resetCredentials` map keyed by the credential string, so `verifyResetCredential` /
      `resetPassword` need no changes — only the value generated and sent for the phone branch
      changes.
    files:
      - src/auth/store.js
    rationale: |
      This is the one place the already-approved design's OTP screen (6-digit, numeric-only
      entry) and the already-built backend diverge: the design and the built UI both commit to a
      6-digit code, but the store never produces one for the phone channel. Without this fix,
      AC2/AC3 are only true for the email channel in practice, not for SMS, even though existing
      tests pass (because they stop short of the real end-to-end boundary).
  - description: |
      Add a regression/characterization test proving the existing phone-channel tests in
      `test/auth.test.js` keep passing unchanged (they assert on `sendResetSms` call args, not
      credential shape, so no edits needed there) and add one new test next to them asserting the
      generated code is realistically typeable.
    files:
      - test/auth.test.js
    rationale: |
      Keeps the regression coverage colocated with the existing AC2/AC3/AC4 phone-channel tests
      in the same file/style (supertest against `src/server.js`, `notifyClient` spied via
      `jest.spyOn`), rather than introducing a new test file for a one-function fix.
  - description: |
      Add a focused unit test in `test/auth-store.test.js` (same file/style as the existing AC6
      `passwordMeetsComplexity` test) asserting `requestPasswordReset` produces a 6-digit numeric
      string for the phone channel and leaves the email channel's UUID format untouched.
    files:
      - test/auth-store.test.js
    rationale: |
      `test/auth-store.test.js` already imports directly from `src/auth/store.js` and resets
      state via `_resetForTests()` in `beforeEach` — the natural home for a store-level unit test
      of credential generation, as opposed to the HTTP-level test in `test/auth.test.js`.
tests:
  - |
    AC1 (already covered, no new test needed): `test/auth.test.js` — `'AC1: unknown contact
    returns the same generic message as a known contact'` already asserts
    `expect(unknown.body.message).toBe(known.body.message)` for `/auth/forgot-password`, and
    `test/forgot-password.test.js` AC1 already asserts the unknown-contact UI path reaches
    `.screen[data-name="confirm"]`. Verified these still pass against the current
    `src/auth/routes.js` / `src/auth/store.js` / `public/js/forgot-password.js` — no changes.
  - |
    AC2 email path (already covered): `test/auth.test.js` `'AC2: a verified email contact
    triggers a reset email send'` asserts
    `expect(spy).toHaveBeenCalledWith(expect.objectContaining({ to: 'works@example.com', ttlMinutes: 15 }))`.
    No changes needed.
  - |
    AC2 phone path — NEW failing test first (the actual gap this plan fixes), added to
    `test/auth.test.js` near the existing `'AC2: a verified phone contact triggers a reset SMS
    send'` test:
    ```js
    test('AC2: the SMS one-time code is a 6-digit numeric string the user can type into the OTP screen', async () => {
      const notifyClient = require('../src/auth/notifyClient');
      let captured;
      jest.spyOn(notifyClient, 'sendResetSms').mockImplementation(async (args) => { captured = args; return args; });
      await request(app).post('/auth/forgot-password').send({ contact: '+1 555 010 0100' });
      expect(captured.credential).toMatch(/^[0-9]{6}$/);
      notifyClient.sendResetSms.mockRestore();
    });
    ```
    This fails today because `captured.credential` is a UUID, not 6 digits. Minimal fix: in
    `requestPasswordReset`, branch the credential generator by `channel`:
    ```js
    const credential = channel === 'email'
      ? crypto.randomUUID()
      : String(crypto.randomInt(0, 1_000_000)).padStart(6, '0');
    ```
  - |
    AC2 phone path — companion unit test in `test/auth-store.test.js`:
    ```js
    test('AC2: requestPasswordReset generates a 6-digit code for phone and a UUID for email', async () => {
      const notifyClient = require('../src/auth/notifyClient');
      let smsArgs, emailArgs;
      jest.spyOn(notifyClient, 'sendResetSms').mockImplementation(async (a) => { smsArgs = a; return a; });
      jest.spyOn(notifyClient, 'sendResetEmail').mockImplementation(async (a) => { emailArgs = a; return a; });
      const { requestPasswordReset } = require('../src/auth/store');
      await requestPasswordReset('+1 555 010 0100');
      await requestPasswordReset('works@example.com');
      expect(smsArgs.credential).toMatch(/^[0-9]{6}$/);
      expect(emailArgs.credential).toMatch(/^[0-9a-f-]{36}$/);
      notifyClient.sendResetSms.mockRestore();
      notifyClient.sendResetEmail.mockRestore();
    });
    ```
  - |
    AC3 (already covered): `test/auth.test.js` `'AC3: a valid unused unexpired credential
    verifies'` posts the captured email credential to `/auth/reset/verify` and asserts
    `expect(res.body.valid).toBe(true)`; `test/forgot-password.test.js` AC3 asserts a `?token=`
    in the URL drives the UI straight to `.screen[data-name="set-password"]`. After this plan's
    fix, the phone-channel equivalent (6-digit code -> `/auth/reset/verify` -> valid) is also now
    true in practice, though no new end-to-end UI test is added for it since the existing
    `forgot-password.test.js` fakes the API boundary deliberately (per its own comments) and a
    real browser-level test of SMS delivery is out of scope for this stack.
  - |
    AC4 (already covered, no changes): `test/auth.test.js` has three AC4 cases — unknown
    credential (`expect(res.status).toBe(400)`), expired via `jest.spyOn(Date, 'now')`, and
    already-used (confirm then re-verify) — and `test/forgot-password.test.js` AC4 asserts a
    rejected `verifyCredential` routes the UI to `.screen[data-name="error"]`.
  - |
    AC5 (already covered, no changes): `test/forgot-password.test.js` `'AC5: the error screen
    offers "Request a new reset link" back to the request screen'` clicks `#error-retry-btn` and
    asserts `.screen[data-name="request"]` is shown, matching `error-retry-btn` wiring in
    `public/js/forgot-password.js` (`showScreen('request')`) and the design's error screen
    (`data-name="Reset link/code error"`, button "Request a new reset link").
  - |
    AC6 (already covered, no changes): `test/auth-store.test.js` AC6 asserts
    `passwordMeetsComplexity` behavior directly; `test/forgot-password.test.js` AC6 asserts a weak
    password leaves `#pw-new-error` visible, `#pw-new` value preserved (`'weak'`), and
    `expect(api.resetPassword).not.toHaveBeenCalled()`.
  - |
    AC7 (already covered, no changes): `test/forgot-password.test.js` AC7 and `test/auth.test.js`
    `'AC7: resetPassword rejects a mismatched confirmation'`
    (`expect(res.status).toBe(422); expect(res.body.error).toBe('password_mismatch')`).
  - |
    AC8 (already covered, no changes): `test/auth.test.js` `'AC8: the new password works for
    subsequent login; the old one no longer does'` asserts both
    `expect(verifyLogin('works@example.com', 'BrandNew1!')).toBe(true)` and
    `expect(verifyLogin('works@example.com', 'OldPassw0rd!')).toBe(false)`.
  - |
    AC9 (already covered, no changes): `test/auth.test.js` AC9
    (`expect(res.body.success).toBe(true)`) and `test/forgot-password.test.js` AC9 asserting
    `.screen[data-name="success"]` becomes visible.
  - |
    AC10 (already covered, no changes): `test/forgot-password.test.js` AC10 asserts, after
    `jest.advanceTimersByTime(3000)`,
    `expect(navigate).toHaveBeenCalledWith('./sign-in.html?resetSuccess=1')`, and
    `test/sign-in.test.js` AC10 asserts `initSignInApp(document, '?resetSuccess=1')` makes
    `#signin-banner` visible, matching the design's sign-in screen banner
    (`.status-icon.is-success` + "Password updated. Sign in with your new password.").
assumptions_or_open_questions:
  - |
    This story's own "open question for planning" (confirm the auth/account system's status) is
    resolved by observation: an auth/account system already exists in this codebase
    (`src/auth/store.js`'s in-memory `users` Map with a seeded `user_4410` / scrypt password
    hashing / `verifyLogin`), and this story's implementation already builds directly on it. No
    separate "build the account system" story was needed or blocking.
  - |
    The in-memory `users` / `resetCredentials` Maps in `src/auth/store.js` (reset on process
    restart, no persistence layer) match this project's existing conventions elsewhere (e.g.
    `src/workflows/store.js`, `src/hires/store.js` use the same in-memory-Map-plus-`_resetForTests`
    pattern) — treated as an accepted project convention, not a gap to fix here.
  - |
    `notifyClient.js`'s `sendResetEmail` / `sendResetSms` are no-op stubs (they don't actually
    send anything, just resolve with an object) — treated as intentionally out of scope: wiring a
    real email/SMS provider is not implied by any AC and isn't part of this fix.
  - |
    Did not add a browser-level end-to-end test that drives the phone/OTP flow through the real
    `/auth` routes with the `maxlength="6"` input, because the existing frontend test suite for
    this screen (`test/forgot-password.test.js`) deliberately mocks the API boundary (see its own
    `fakeApi` helper) rather than hitting `src/server.js`; the new `test/auth.test.js` /
    `test/auth-store.test.js` tests cover the credential-format contract the UI depends on
    instead. Flagging this as a boundary worth a reviewer's judgment call rather than silently
    picking a side.
  - |
    Did not treat the small timing difference in `requestPasswordReset` (it `await`s
    `notifyClient.send*` only when a user is found, returning immediately otherwise) as an AC1
    defect, since the current `notifyClient` stubs resolve synchronously with no real latency, so
    there is no observable timing side-channel today. Flagging it here in case a reviewer wants it
    addressed proactively before a real provider is wired in, but it is not part of this plan's
    scope since no AC calls for it and fixing it well (constant-time response) is nontrivial.
package_dependencies: []
notes: |
  Design verification: read `.arc/designs/TEST-M1-STORY-095-design.html` in full. It defines 7
  screens (`Sign in`, `Forgot password – Request`, `Check your email or phone`, `Enter reset
  code`, `Reset link/code error`, `Set new password`, `Password updated`) using the shared dark
  purple/violet token set (`--color-primary: #a855f7`, `--color-bg: #181021`, etc.), `.auth-card`
  / `.field` / `.checklist` / `.status-icon` / `.otp-input` components, and an inline demo script
  that is explicitly prototype-only (`.demo-panel`, "not part of the real product"). The already
  built `public/forgot-password.html`, `public/sign-in.html`, and `public/css/auth.css` reproduce
  every one of those real (non-demo-panel) screens, ids, and classes faithfully — same `data-name`
  screen labels (minus the demo panel), same `#otp-input` 6-digit numeric constraint, same
  checklist rule set (`len`/`upper`/`lower`/`num`/`special`), same TTL copy (15 min email / 10 min
  SMS) backed by `EMAIL_TTL_MINUTES` / `SMS_TTL_MINUTES` in `src/auth/store.js`, and the same
  sign-in success banner. No UI work remains; this plan does not add or touch any screen markup.

  The only real engineering gap found versus both the design and the stated ACs is the SMS
  credential-format bug described in `scope` above. Call flow for the fix:

  ```mermaid
  flowchart TD
    routes["src/auth/routes.js\nPOST /auth/forgot-password"] --> store["src/auth/store.js\nrequestPasswordReset(contact)"]
    store --> notify["src/auth/notifyClient.js\nsendResetSms / sendResetEmail"]
    ui["public/js/forgot-password.js\n#otp-input (maxlength=6, digits only)"] -->|POST credential| verify["src/auth/routes.js\nPOST /auth/reset/verify"]
    verify --> store
    classDef touched fill:#f96,color:#000
    class store touched
  ```

  `store` is the only touched node — it is the single place that both produces the SMS credential
  (consumed by `notify`) and later checks whatever the OTP UI submits (via `verify`), which is why
  fixing the generator there alone closes the gap without touching `routes.js`, `notifyClient.js`,
  or the frontend.
review_focus: |
  Scope is intentionally narrow: this plan does NOT re-build or re-test anything already covered
  by `test/auth.test.js`, `test/auth-store.test.js`, `test/forgot-password.test.js`, or
  `test/sign-in.test.js` — those already pass and already satisfy AC1, AC3-AC10. The only in-scope
  change is making `requestPasswordReset`'s phone-channel credential a 6-digit numeric string
  instead of a UUID, so the SMS "one-time code" the design and built OTP screen promise is
  actually satisfiable. Riskiest spot: `resetCredentials` is still a single `Map` keyed by
  whatever string is generated, so a reviewer should confirm the 6-digit phone code and the UUID
  email token can't collide in a way that matters (they can't in practice — formats are
  disjoint-enough and this is a single-process in-memory store — but it's worth a second look
  since both now share one keyspace). Do not flag the lack of a real email/SMS provider or the
  in-memory store as bugs — both are pre-existing, deliberate conventions in this codebase, not
  introduced or left unfinished by this plan.
